DUP(2)                         P32W Unix                          DUP(2)

NAME
       dup, dup2 - duplicate a file descriptor

SYNOPSIS
       #include <unistd.h>

       int dup(int fd);
       int dup2(int fd, int fd2);

DESCRIPTION
       dup returns a new file descriptor, the lowest one free,
       referring to the same open file as fd. The two share the
       file offset and the access mode; closing one leaves the
       other open.

       dup2 makes fd2 refer to the open file of fd, first closing
       whatever fd2 had open. If fd2 equals fd, nothing happens.
       A shell uses it for redirection:

              fd = open("out", O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, 0666);
              dup2(fd, 1);
              close(fd);

RETURN VALUE
       The new descriptor (dup2: fd2); -1 on an error, with errno
       set.

ERRORS
       EBADF   fd is not an open file descriptor; or (dup2) fd2
               is not between 0 and 19.
       EMFILE  (dup) all 20 descriptors are in use.

SEE ALSO
       open(2), close(2), pipe(2), fork(2), sh(1)
